    <!-- EXPANDED EXPLANATION -->
    <div class="explanation">
        <p>
            The numbers above tell the whole story. A single 800mm² monolithic
            die has nowhere to hide a defect; one particle anywhere on the
            silicon scraps the entire chip, dropping yield to 0%. In 2015, with
            TSMC's 16nm wafers costing roughly $8,000 each, that meant a  <strong
                >$10,000+</strong
            > processor was reduced to scrap by a single microscopic speck of dust.
        </p>
        <p>
            Chiplets change the math entirely. By splitting the same functional
            area into 16 smaller, independently fabricated dies (each ~50mm²), a
            single defect only takes out the one chiplet it lands on. The other
            15 are still perfectly usable, pushing the net functional yield up
            to 93.75%. This is why AMD's EPYC processors could scale to 64 cores
            while Intel's monolithic Xeons were stuck at 28 cores in the same
            generation; the cost structure simply didn't allow larger dies.
        </p>
        <p>
            This is the core economic insight behind AMD's chiplet strategy.
            Smaller dies are dramatically cheaper to manufacture at high yield
            because defect probability scales with area; cutting the area by
            16x, and you cut your exposure to random particles by the same
            factor. By 2023, AMD had refined this approach further with 5nm
            chiplets and a 6nm I/O die, achieving up to 128 cores per socket
            while maintaining industry-leading yield rates.
        </p>
    </div>
